WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:04.242
Quincy and Becca sat in the dark classroom, fanning themselves.

2
00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:09.988
The other students had all gone home because of the blackout, but Becca's parents couldn't pick her up early.

3
00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:15.415
Quincy had forgotten his house key, so he was stuck at school too, until his mom came.

4
00:00:15.416 --> 00:00:19.113
Mrs. DeWitt sat by the window, grading papers.

5
00:00:19.114 --> 00:00:22.912
"At least we got to skip the debates today," Quincy said.

6
00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:26.394
"Don't worry," Mrs. DeWitt said with a smile.

7
00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:29.300
"We'll hold the debates tomorrow."

8
00:00:29.301 --> 00:00:31.932
"I like to debate," Becca said.

9
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:36.538
"But it's so hard to prepare when we don't know the topic ahead of time."

10
00:00:36.539 --> 00:00:44.988
Mrs. DeWitt nodded. "It is, but I want you to think of strong arguments on the spot.

11
00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:51.019
Everyone has opinions—people should know how to support their opinions."

12
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:56.183
"I definitely have an opinion about this blackout," Quincy groaned.

13
00:00:56.184 --> 00:01:00.676
"Being without power is boring and hot!"

14
00:01:00.677 --> 00:01:10.458
"The utility company asked residents to conserve energy during the heat wave so we wouldn't have a blackout," Mrs. DeWitt said.

15
00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:16.274
"But people must be using more power than the system can handle."

16
00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:20.319
Becca had been studying renewable energy in science.

17
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.008
She remembered something that she had read.

18
00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:28.396
"People use less household electricity now than they did ten years ago.

19
00:01:28.397 --> 00:01:32.316
Appliances and technology are more energy efficient."

20
00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:37.420
"Oh?" Mrs. DeWitt said. "Interesting . . . "

21
00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:43.493
"Well, I think we need more power plants to produce more energy," Quincy burst out.

22
00:01:43.494 --> 00:01:49.327
"The problem is, most power plants burn fossil fuels like coal, oil, or natural gas.

23
00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:51.523
And that's bad for the environment."

24
00:01:51.524 --> 00:01:55.053
"So what's the solution?" Mrs. DeWitt asked.

25
00:01:55.054 --> 00:01:58.988
"More people should use solar power!" Becca declared.

26
00:01:58.989 --> 00:02:03.041
"It's clean, renewable energy.

27
00:02:03.042 --> 00:02:09.625
In fact, enough solar energy hits Earth in one hour to provide all the power we need for a year.

28
00:02:09.626 --> 00:02:14.872
With solar, families could cover all their household electricity needs."

29
00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:20.044
"Solar panels are expensive, and so is installing them," Quincy pointed out.

30
00:02:20.045 --> 00:02:22.624
"Plus, the panels aren't efficient.

31
00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:27.746
Only some of the sun's energy that hits them gets converted into electricity."

32
00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:31.977
"What's your idea, Quincy?" Mrs. DeWitt asked.

33
00:02:31.978 --> 00:02:38.012
Quincy thought for a moment. "Wind and water are renewable sources of energy, unlike fossil fuels.

34
00:02:38.013 --> 00:02:44.648
And they don't produce air pollution. We could build more wind turbines and dams."

35
00:02:44.649 --> 00:02:49.736
Becca shook her head. "Dams take up a lot of space and disrupt the environment.

36
00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:52.670
Even wind farms disturb the land's surface.

37
00:02:52.671 --> 00:02:58.151
These forms of power can only be built in places with water or wind." She grinned.

38
00:02:58.152 --> 00:03:01.091
"Solar panels don't disrupt the environment.

39
00:03:01.092 --> 00:03:07.014
And they can be installed anywhere the sun shines—like on peoples' roofs."

40
00:03:07.015 --> 00:03:12.036
"True, but people need a lot of panels to cover their entire usage.

41
00:03:12.037 --> 00:03:17.729
They also need a battery system to store energy for when the sun isn't shining," Quincy said.

42
00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:23.052
"Dams and wind farms can produce larger amounts of electricity than solar."

43
00:03:23.053 --> 00:03:25.587
"I see your point," Becca said.

44
00:03:25.588 --> 00:03:30.432
"But if more people use solar, we could reduce our need for big power plants."

45
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:36.755
"Wouldn't it be better to wait until costs go down and the technology is more efficient?" Quincy asked.

46
00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:40.857
"Nope! We need more solar power now," Becca said.

47
00:03:40.858 --> 00:03:46.841
"The more people use solar, the more costs will go down and the technology will improve."

48
00:03:46.842 --> 00:03:51.412
Mrs. DeWitt began to clap. "Great job, you two."

49
00:03:51.413 --> 00:03:55.834
The kids looked at her, confused.

50
00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.212
"With what?" Becca asked.

51
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:02.967
"With your arguments about renewable energy," the teacher said.

52
00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:07.463
"You each earned an A on tomorrow's debate assignment."

53
00:04:07.464 --> 00:04:10.898
"Yes!" Quincy sat back with a grin.

54
00:04:10.899 --> 00:04:14.954
But Becca leaned forward. "Who won?"

55
00:04:14.955 --> 00:04:17.862
"It was a tie," Mrs. DeWitt said.

56
00:04:17.863 --> 00:04:23.411
"You both presented clear opinions and supported them with strong evidence.

57
00:04:23.412 --> 00:04:28.141
That's exactly what skilled debaters do."

58
00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:33.481
Just then the classroom phone buzzed.

59
00:04:33.482 --> 00:04:39.469
Mrs. DeWitt picked it up and spoke to the school secretary for a moment.

60
00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.238
"Quincy, your mom is waiting at the main office."

61
00:04:43.239 --> 00:04:46.890
"Great!"

62
00:04:46.891 --> 00:04:49.639
He grabbed his things and headed toward the door.

63
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.063
"Stay cool!" Becca said.

64
00:04:52.064 --> 00:04:54.413
"I will." Quincy grinned.

65
00:04:54.414 --> 00:05:02.092
"At home we're prepared for blackouts. We have backup solar power!"

